{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016ioqw7VDgLowHSFsboJVc2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"APC 100 has 360 AIRs, which is >= 200, so it uses multi-threading. Round 0: 464→469ms. That's no improvement!\n\nWait, APC 100 has 360 AIRs which is >= 200. Let me check. But the Round 0 is 469ms, actually slightly worse than before (464ms). That's strange because APC 300 (623 AIRs) showed massive improvement (669→349ms).\n\nHmm, wait. Let me think about this. APC 100 has 360 AIRs, but maybe the distribution of AIR sizes is different. \n\nActually, I'm realizing the threshold might be the issue here. APC 100 has 360 AIRs across 3 segments, so each segment only gets around 120 AIRs—below the 200 threshold. That would explain why multi-threading isn't helping, whereas APC 300's 2 segments each have enough AIRs to benefit. the threshold to something like 100 to capture APC 100. Looking at the per-segment AIR distribution, APC 0 has around 20 AIRs per segment, APC 100 has roughly 120, and APC 300 has about 300. With a threshold of 200, only APC 300 would trigger multi-threading, leaving APC 100 without improvement. Lowering to 100 would help, though I'm concerned about whether medium-sized segments would actually benefit from the threading overhead.\n\nThe LogUp GKR regression I saw at APC 0 was likely from multi-threading polluting the CUDA memory pool, but that disappeared when I sequentialized at threshold 200. Now I need to find the right balance—a threshold of 100 should include APC 100 for parallelization while keeping APC 0 sequential.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":550,"cache_read_input_tokens":99372,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":550},"output_tokens":13,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_to